WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:08.120
There are many problems that differ in
meaning, and yet

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:13.900
there are mathematical models that let us
solve them in the same way.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:20.720
For example, there is a bank account that
changes every few days,

4
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:27.820
and its balance keeps growing. You need to
find how fast the account grows.

5
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.340
If the position of a car is known

6
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:37.140
and it moves along a straight line, we need to
find its speed at a certain moment t.

7
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:43.720
We are given the graph of a function, and

8
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.508
tangent lines are drawn to it at some points;

9
00:00:46.508 --> 00:00:50.660
we need to find the tangent of the slope angle
of the tangent line.

10
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:57.680
Our problems are stated quite differently, and
it seems they must be solved

11
00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.840
in completely different ways, but
mathematicians

12
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:07.060
figured out exactly how to solve all of them
using the derivative.

13
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:15.780
Let a function y = f(x) be given in some
region, together with a point M

14
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:22.460
in its domain. We give the argument an
increment Δx and

15
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:28.980
find the corresponding value of the function
f(x + Δx). We find Δy,

16
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.260
the increment of the function.

17
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:36.820
We find the ratio of the increment of the
function to the increment of the argument.

18
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.219
The limit of the ratio of the increment of the
function to

19
00:01:42.219 --> 00:01:45.580
the increment of the argument as the latter
tends to zero

20
00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:53.580
is called the derivative of the function and
is denoted y′ or dy/dx.

21
00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.714
Finding a derivative is called
differentiation.

22
00:02:00.714 --> 00:02:03.770
Geometrically, the derivative of y = f(x)

23
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:10.510
is the slope of the tangent to the graph of
this function.

24
00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.804
From the point of view of mechanics,

25
00:02:12.804 --> 00:02:16.900
the derivative is the speed of straight-line
motion at any moment.

26
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.603
In general, the derivative of any function
expresses

27
00:02:20.603 --> 00:02:23.380
the rate of change of that function. If

28
00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.860
the function itself describes some process,

29
00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:31.100
its derivative is the rate of that process.

30
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.184
Now let us state the basic rules of
differentiation.

31
00:02:37.184 --> 00:02:40.250
Let c be a constant, and let the functions u

32
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.250
and v have derivatives.

33
00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.767
First, the derivative of a constant is zero.

34
00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.960
Second, the derivative of the independent

35
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:57.690
variable x equals one. Third, the derivative
of a sum

36
00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:04.550
or difference of functions equals the sum or
difference of their derivatives.

37
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:12.050
Fourth, a constant factor can be taken outside
the derivative sign.

38
00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:16.740
Fifth, the derivative of the product of two
functions

39
00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:23.580
equals the derivative of the first function
times the second, plus the first

40
00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.640
function times the derivative of the second.

41
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:35.810
Sixth, the derivative of a quotient of two
functions: multiply u′ by v,

42
00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:42.770
subtract u times v′, and divide this
difference by v².

43
00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.154
The derivative of a fraction equals the
difference between

44
00:03:46.154 --> 00:03:49.100
the derivative of the numerator times the
denominator

45
00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:55.260
and the numerator times the derivative of the
denominator,

46
00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.040
divided by the square of the denominator.

47
00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.017
Based on the definition of the derivative,

48
00:04:03.017 --> 00:04:06.800
a special table of derivatives of elementary
functions has been compiled.

49
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.720
With it we can find the derivatives of power,
logarithmic,

50
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.420
trigonometric and hyperbolic functions.

51
00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.793
Let us look at a differentiation rule in an
example.

52
00:04:22.793 --> 00:04:25.340
Note that the fraction contains only power

53
00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:32.150
functions, so we transform it. For this, let
us recall from school

54
00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:38.760
mathematics the rule for operations with
powers: if

55
00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.279
powers have the same base, their exponents can

56
00:04:43.279 --> 00:04:47.700
be added, subtracted, divided and multiplied.

57
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.620
Let us transform our fraction.

58
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.960
First we write the cube root of x as the power
one third, and the square

59
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.693
root as one half. Next we split the fraction
into

60
00:05:03.693 --> 00:05:06.740
two fractions with the same denominator.

61
00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:13.480
Now we transform it and get the difference of
two power functions.

62
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.960
Now let us find the derivative of the
resulting expression.

63
00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:22.524
Let us look at another example of finding a
derivative:

64
00:05:22.524 --> 00:05:25.230
y equals x to the seventh power times arc-

65
00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.625
tangent of x. We find the derivative of the
product

66
00:05:28.625 --> 00:05:31.820
of two functions. By the rule for the
derivative

67
00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:35.024
of a product, we first find the derivative of

68
00:05:35.024 --> 00:05:37.800
the first function, then of the second,

69
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:45.620
taking them from the table of derivatives. The
final answer is written like this.

70
00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:54.280
Let us look at one more example of finding the
derivative of a fraction:

71
00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:00.400
y equals the natural logarithm of x divided by
x². We have rules for

72
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:06.710
differentiating fractions. Using the table of
derivatives for power and logarithmic

73
00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:13.350
functions, we get a fraction in which the x's
cancel,

74
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:20.660
and we get the following expression. We take x
outside the brackets.

75
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.160
We cancel x again and get the answer.

76
00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:31.049
Let us sum up the lesson. To find a
derivative,

77
00:06:31.049 --> 00:06:33.970
break the expression under the prime sign

78
00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:40.830
into elementary functions and determine by
which operations these functions

79
00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:47.580
are linked: product, sum or quotient. Then

80
00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.813
we find the derivatives of the elementary
functions in the table

81
00:06:50.813 --> 00:06:53.440
of derivatives, and the formulas for the
derivatives

82
00:06:53.440 --> 00:07:00.600
of a product, sum and quotient in the rules of
differentiation.
